Arrow functions:

- Before jumping into arrow function, first, let's see a few ways of writing function.
    
    1) Function declaration(classic):
            
                function square(a) {
                    return a * a;
                }
        -> Hoisted (can be used before definition)
        -> Has its own this
    
    2) Function expression:

                const square = function (a) {               // anonymous function expression
                    return a * a;
                };
        -> Not hoisted like declarations
        -> Function is stored in a variable
        -> Can be anonymous or named

                const square = function squareNumbers(a) {     // named function expression
                    return a * a;
                };

    3) Arrow funtion:
        -> remove the function keyword by adding fat arrow
                const square = (a) => {
                    return a * a;
                }

        -> Omit the parentheses in the function parameters when the signature contains one parameter
            only.
                const square = a => {
                    return a * a;
                }

        -> Omit the curly braces in the function body and the return keyword if the function has
            only one statement
                const square = a => a * a;
           

- Let's take our previous example with promises:
            
            getUser(login)
                .then(function (user) {
                    return getRights(user);             // getRights is returning a promise
                })
                .then(function (rights) {
                    return updateMenu(rights);
                })
    
    now, this can be written with arrow functions like:

            getUser(login)
                .then(user => getRights(user))
                .then(rights => updateMenu(rights))

- Arrow function has a special trick, a great power over normal functions: the this stays lexically
    bounded, which means that these functions don’t have a new this as other functions do. 

            var maxFinder = {
                max: 0,
                find: function (numbers) {
                    // let's iterate
                    numbers.forEach(function (element) {
                        // if the element is greater, set it as the max
                        if (element > this.max) {
                            this.max = element;
                        }
                    });
                }
            };
            maxFinder.find([2, 3, 4]);
            console.log(maxFinder.max);

    ⚠️You would expect this to work, but it doesn’t. WHY THIS DOESN'T WORK?
    => There is a forEach callback function inside a find function. Each regular function will have
        its own this and notice that the forEach in the find function uses this, but the this is
        not bound to an object. So this.max is not the max of the maxFinder object… Of course you can fix
        it easily, 
        ✅ using an alias:

            var maxFinder = {
                max: 0,
                find: function (numbers) {
                    var self = this;
                    numbers.forEach(function (element) {
                        if (element > self.max) {
                            self.max = element;
                        }
                    });
                }
            };
            maxFinder.find([2, 3, 4]);
            // log the result
            console.log(maxFinder.max);
        

        ✅ or binding the this:

            var maxFinder = {
                max: 0,
                find: function (numbers) {
                    numbers.forEach(function (element) {
                        if (element > this.max) {
                            this.max = element;
                        }
                    }.bind(this));
                }
            };
            maxFinder.find([2, 3, 4]);
            // log the result
            console.log(maxFinder.max);


        ✅ But there is now an even more elegant solution with the arrow function syntax:

            var maxFinder = {
                max: 0,
                find: function (numbers) {
                    numbers.forEach(element => {
                        if (element > this.max) {
                            this.max = element;
                        }
                    });
                }
            };
            maxFinder.find([2, 3, 4]);
            // log the result
            console.log(maxFinder.max);